Robin Hanson and I seem to be using different definitions for the term
"coercion."  Quoting my Funk & Wagnells:  1. Forcible constraint or
restraint, moral or physical.  2. Government by force.
 
I assumed, incorrectly as it turns out, that we were both talking about
physical force.  If we were, then I am correct and Robin is incorrect--Mock
Courts, by definition, cannot be coercive.  If, instead, we speak of moral
coercion, then I am wrong.
 
I see now that I should have qualified my arguments with the more complete
term:  physical coercion.  Thanks, Robin, for non-physically forcing me to
improve my clarity.
 
Who designed this language, anyway?  It's a pain in the butt having multiple
definitions for the same word!  :-( 
 
