> Wasn't the swastika used by some ancient culture (egyptians?) as 
> a symbol for the sun?  This certainly pre-dates its use to signify
> german fascism.  I don't remember the details but I think the german
> swastika and the sun symbol differed in the orientation of the "hands".
> 
> --
> Yanek Martinson

Prior to National Socialism, the swastika was considered a negative, 
destructive symbol.  Hitler, who was highly mystical and Occultist (he would
fit well into the "New Age" crowd, vegetarianism, astrology...), reversed the
direction of the arrows, thinking that it would bring good fortune to his
cause.

Despite its faults, Leonard Peikoff's The Ominous Parallel provides a great 
deal of fascinating information regarding Hitler's semi-Christian/mystical
beliefs, in addition to showing how the German culture was steeped in
collectivist and anti-rationalist philosophy.

The five-arrow spiral logo (i.e., the current version) looks quite unlike a
swastika, so I hope that extremely anti-extropian association will be avoided.

