I'd snort in derision, but I once heard that Joe Sixpack can't tell the
Chrysler "pentastar" from the ARCo symbol (which resembles 4/5 of a
pentastar).
 
> Wasn't the swastika used by some ancient culture (egyptians?) as 
> a symbol for the sun?  This certainly pre-dates its use to signify
> german fascism.  I don't remember the details but I think the german
> swastika and the sun symbol differed in the orientation of the "hands".
 
I think various cultures have used both versions.  But here is the origin I
was told for the name "swastika":  
	In India, if the god accepts a sacrifice (burnt offering), the smoke
is seen to bend to the right.  "It is good [su asti]."  ("su" being related,
it seems, to the Greek "(h)eu-".)  The su-asti-ka, then, is a sign composed
of rightward-turnings.
 
I visited the Carlsberg brewery in Copenhagen in '75.  Its gates are guarded
by stone elephants each of which rests a foot on a ball adorned with a
swastika.  I once had a copy of the Danish-Esperanto phrasebook that
Carlsberg passed out for the Universala Esperanto-Kongreso, the two times it
was in Copenhagen (~1906 and 1975):  its cover showed the Carlsberg sun,
complete with swastika, shining on Copenhagen.  (Carlsberg's swastika is
distinguished by four dots between the arms.)
 
I think Carlsberg *and* Hitler used the clockwise version.
 
