Date: Thu, 31 Dec 92 15:52:49 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re: META--MARKET SOLUTION

Sandy Sandfroft writes:
>Here's the point:  Paying to post, encourages you to construct
>your arguments as economically as possible.  It's sort of like
>sending a telegram; you want to get the maximum bang for your
>buck.  Without such a constraint, the tendency is toward bloated,
>sloppy writing, and specious, shoot-from-the-hip reasoning (none
>dare call it intellectual masturbation).

Look, there is a more principled way to do this analysis.  Let's
imagine that we could negotiate prices for each post, and ask what
those prices would be.  Either the readers would end up paying the
poster for the delight of reading, or the poster would end up paying
the reader for the extra burden of more messages to wade through.

If we're going to add money transfers, what we want is to better
approximate those ideal prices, the ones would would agree to if we
could think about it a bunch.  If, on average, a particular person
would end up paying to post, then it is better if they have to pay a
standard fee to post.  If, however, that person would end up getting
paid on average, then it would be better if they got paid a standard
amount to post.  

So your proposal will improve the incentives for posters whom readers
are not too kean on reading, but would make incentives worse for the
posters we all love to read.  These people are now given too
little incentive to post, and your proposal makes this problem worse.

So maybe votes of approval or disapproval should give a monetary
amount you would have been willing to pay, or should have been paid to
read the post.  Then the charge/reward per post could be based on
historical statistics for that person.  But once real money gets
involved, there become stronger incentives for folks to not be
entirely honest in their votes.  There may well be something that
might work along these lines, but it needs more thought.

Robin Hanson 

