Date: Thu, 31 Dec 92 16:55:17 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed? 

Imagine a future where our decendants are so rich and powerful that if
only a small fraction of them want to restructure the galaxy to make
it more to their liking, and were unopposed by the rest, then they
could do it within a few hundred million years.

I take this to be a plausible "extropian" image of where "boundless
expansion" might lead us.  And I think most of us find in plausible
(at least a one percent chance) that we will actually have such a
future.  As long as some of us want to keep growing we come to
dominate the rest, etc.

However, I think one can similarly argue the plausibility that life
should have arisen on our planet, and should have eventually lead to
intelligent life with a growing economy.  Nothing that we know of that
happened in our history seems particularly implausible.

Problem is, the above two plausibilities are together dramatically
contradicted by the data.  With probably more than a hundred billion
stars in our galaxy, and probably a similar number of planets, none
has yet dramatically restructured the galaxy.  And there has been
plenty of time.  If we're going to do it we'll probably do it in the
next ten million years (if not the next hundred thousand!), yet there
is no reason why life elsewhere couldn't have reached our level a
billion or more years ago.  It would have just needed a star that was
born a little earlier, and/or asteroid impacts or accidents of
evolution to have happened a little earlier.

Worse, we can look out farther into the universe, observing many
millions of galaxies (with billions more out there), and have yet to
see anything that looks less than natural.  As far as we can see, the
whole universe looks natural, with incredible amounts of useful mass
locked up in stars or galactic nuclei, "pissing away" useful
negentropy into the night sky.  Sure, advanced folks might leave
nature preserves, or might be a little less visible than other stuff
-- but we don't see *anything* suspicious.

So there is clearly some terribly strong filter, making it very
improbable (less than one in a million billion) that any particular
planet will grow to restructure a galaxy (or a galaxy group, or ...).
I call this the extropian filter.  And an incredibly crucial question
to ask is: how far along this filter are we?  Even if we had passed
through a factor of a trillion of the filter, this would mean we have
at least another factor of a thousand to go!  Leaving more than a
999/1000 chance from here of *not* making it to a level where we'd
have the ability to remake our galaxy.

Yes one might construct scenarios where any one civilization might
choose not to mess with their galaxy, even if they had the ability to
(say by mostly congregating about a central black hole), but this
should be small comfort.  Even if only one percent of such capable
civilizations actually do such remaking (over a billion year period)
that only makes the true filter a factor of one hundred less than it
otherwise appears to be.

Two conclusions follow from this, I think.  First, by learning about
our past evolution, and just how improbable it was, we can learn very
important things about our distant future.  Similar information comes
from looking out farther and in more detail into space.  We hope to
fine that our past evolution was very improbable, and to see other
success stories way our there, but wishing don't make it so.

Second, we should take very seriously the possibility that the odds
are very high against us; take disaster scenarios seriously, such as
nuclear war or ecological crash, even if they seem implausible at
first.  Yes, it is anti-extropians, those who seem the most against
such a future for us, that seem to most emphasize such scenarios.  But
granting the strong possibility of failure need not mean endorsing the
remedies the advise, such as state restrictions on economic growth.
Maybe growing fast is the best way to avoid whatever might go wrong.
Or maybe not, we really need to think about this some more.

