Date: Thu, 31 Dec 92 22:30:16 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re:  How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

Robin's post is an interesting variant on Fermi's paradox, the "where are
the aliens" question, given our own projected ability to spread through
the galaxy (and the rest of the universe).

I would contest two of Robin's premises, though.  The first is that there
is nothing implausible about the formation of life on earth.  I am no
expert on this, but I read a book a few years ago, called "Origins"
(don't have the author handy), in which all the major competing
theories for the chemical origins of life were examined, and found
wanting.  In particular, the notion that a single RNA or DNA molecule
could have formed naturally through inorganic processes, and proceeded
to reproduce itself, is pretty much impossible.  A solution may yet be
found that has no obvious holes in it, but presently there is nothing
that everyone can accept.

The other disagreement I have is that observationally we can pretty much
rule out large-scale engineering of any considerable part of the visible
universe.  There are many _very_ weird galaxies out there.  Chip Arp has
made a career out of collecting pathological galaxies.  No doubt most of
these will eventually be explainable through natural processes.  But it
is certainly conceivable that some are due to engineering.  It's not
really clear what we would accept as a marker of an "engineered" galaxy.
Suggestions?

(The other point, as I think Robin mentioned, is that probably the most
sensible engineering to do to a galaxy is to convert each star into a
Dyson sphere, which makes it disappear, at least in visible light.  So,
we might predict that a large fraction of gravitational mass would be
unobservable, hidden behind solar collectors.  Sure enough, most mass is
missing, although again the conventional assumption is that it is in
naturally-forming brown dwarfs or exotic subatomic particles.)

One interesting corollary to Robin's point is that we should _hope_ that
it turns out to be hard to explain how life on Earth got started.  The
less likely we were to have gotten this far, the more of Robin's
extropian filter we have overcome.  If life turns out to start and
evolve easily, we should... be afraid, be very afraid.  In this regard I
suppose we should all cheer the negative results of the Viking
experiments on Mars, and watch with trepidation the outcome of similar
experiments in the future.

